- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。
- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。
- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。
- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。

参考题目如下：
<reference_puzzle index="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="8f215267" lines="7-21">
<description>
Count same-color patterns per frame and represent the tally as dots inside.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Hollow rectangular frames in various colors.
- Small monochrome pattern sprites scattered outside frames.

Rule:
- Remove all small patterns from the grid.
- For each frame of color C, count how many patterns share color C.
- Draw that many dots along the frame's center row, spaced by 2, starting from the right.

Key insight: Frames act as tally displays — scattered same-color patterns are "absorbed" and their count is shown as interior dots.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="eval" puzzle_id="db695cfb" lines="8-19">
<description>
Connect same-color diagonal pixel pairs, then emit perpendicular rays from differently-colored pixels on those diagonals.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Line color (e.g. blue) has pixel pairs aligned on shared diagonals. Trigger color (e.g. magenta) pixels sit along those diagonals.

Rule:
- Draw diagonal lines connecting each pair of same-color aligned pixels.
- Where the diagonal crosses a trigger pixel, emit perpendicular diagonal rays (in the trigger's color) extending to the grid boundary.

Key insight: Trigger pixels act as beam-splitters — the perpendicular rays rotate 90° from the connecting diagonal's orientation.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="eval" puzzle_id="135a2760" lines="6-19">
<description>
Repair noisy periodic patterns inside frame-delimited grid regions.

Input structure:
- Frame color (most common) forms full rows/cols, dividing the grid into rectangular regions.
- bg (second most common) fills empty space; each region contains a small colored tile repeated periodically with a few corrupted pixels.

Rule:
- For each region, find the bounding box of non-bg content.
- Infer the smallest repeating tile that explains the pattern (majority vote per residue position), requiring it repeat at least 2x per axis.
- Replace the region with the clean tiled version.

Key insight: Tile dimensions are unknown and must be inferred; prefer the smallest tile with genuine repetition over one that memorizes the entire region.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="0934a4d8" lines="75-90">
<description>
Reveal the rectangular patch hidden by the solid marker using the grid's reflection symmetries.

Input structure:
- A square colored pattern with one solid rectangular marker color occluding a patch.
- The visible pattern has horizontal and vertical reflection symmetry on a large lower/right strip.
- The top and left outer strips also expose transpose counterparts for cells not recoverable by those reflections.

Rule:
- Find the unique color whose full extent is a solid rectangle; this rectangle is the output region.
- Infer the reflected strip start as the smallest row/column offset whose lower/right strips are mirror-consistent, ignoring marker pixels.
- For each marker pixel, copy the unique non-marker value in its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; if that orbit is entirely hidden in the outer strip, use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart.

Key insight:
The marker is not part of the pattern; it covers a missing rectangle whose cells are still determined by the visible mirror or transpose copies elsewhere in the grid.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="62593bfd" lines="32-46">
<description>
Sort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.

Rule:
- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).
- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).
- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).
- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.

Key insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="71e489b6" lines="7-19">
<description>
Highlight black defects in blue foreground by surrounding them with orange.

Input structure:
- Blue (1) foreground on black (0) bg; small black holes or protrusions within blue regions; occasional thin blue spurs into black areas.

Rule:
- Thin blue pixels mostly surrounded by black are absorbed into the background.
- Small black components (≤3 cells) and leaf pixels of larger black components are "defects."
- Each defect gets an orange (7) 8-neighbor halo; the defect pixels themselves stay black.

Key insight: Only structural anomalies—tiny isolated patches and dangling endpoints—are marked, not bulk black regions.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="9bbf930d" lines="61-72">
<description>
Move magenta particles along corridors formed by parallel colored bars.

Input structure:
- bg=7. Particles=6 in col 0. Colored bars on even rows form corridors on odd rows.

Rule:
- Particle goes straight through bg cells.
- When blocked by a colored cell, turns 90° into the perpendicular direction
  that has a bg cell flanked by same-color bars. Stops at grid edge or full blockage.

Key insight: Same-color parallel bars define the only valid corridors, so each particle follows a deterministic local trace.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="a251c730" lines="8-21">
<description>
Stamp source sprites into a destination frame via color-matched singleton markers.

Input structure:
- Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers).
- Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.

Rule:
- Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color.
- Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position.
- Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.

Key insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="195c6913" lines="37-52">
<description>
Simulate particles along a path with alternating turn direction at collisions.

Input structure:
- Top-left: 2x2 monochrome blocks define a repeating color cycle (left-to-right).
- Isolated single pixels of a cycle color: start markers adjacent to the path.
- A 2x2 monochrome block of a distinct color: end marker.

Rule:
- Each start marker launches a particle moving straight, painting cycle colors in order.
- On hitting a boundary, paint that cell with the end color and turn 90°.
- Turn direction alternates: odd collisions → left (CCW), even → right (CW).
- If the scheduled turn is blocked, the particle stops.

Key insight: The alternating left/right turn rule is the core mechanism; without it the path would be trivial.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="3a25b0d8" lines="9-22">
<description>
Fill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.
- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.

Rule:
- Find the two 8-connected objects.
- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).
- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.

Key insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="5961cc34" lines="8-21">
<description>
Build a red ray network by activating blue blobs via directional green ports.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Large blue blobs with adjacent green pixels marking port directions.
- Start marker: yellow pixel atop a short red segment at grid bottom.

Rule:
- Extend a ray upward from start; on hitting a blue blob, recolor it red.
- Activated blobs emit thick rays through green ports until boundary or another blob.
- Cascade until no new blobs are reached.

Key insight: Green pixels encode ray direction and width; the network activates as a chain reaction from one start point.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="898e7135" lines="7-20">
<description>
Inflate a frame's hole-layout and fill each hole with its matching piece.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Largest component is a monochrome frame enclosing bg holes.
- Outside pieces composed of k×k blocks; k = side of smallest solid square piece.

Rule:
- Extract connected hole regions inside the frame as slots.
- Match each piece to a slot by comparing block-level shapes under rotation/flip.
- Output the layout scaled by k, with pieces placed in slots; frame color as background.

Key insight: Pieces are k×k-block-scaled versions of holes; downsampling reveals shape matches under all 8 orientations.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="7666fa5d" lines="12-26">
<description>
Fill the parallelogram-shaped region between parallel anti-diagonal line segments with red.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- One non-bg color forms short diagonal segments, each lying on a single anti-diagonal (r+c = const).
- Segments come in parallel pairs bounding a region to be filled.

Rule:
- For each main-diagonal axis (r−c = const), collect anti-diagonal segments whose span covers that axis.
- Sort those segments by their anti-diagonal index (r+c).
- Fill all bg cells lying strictly between consecutive segment pairs along that axis with red (color 2).

Key insight: The two diagonal directions decouple — segments sit on anti-diagonals, and filling sweeps along main diagonals between them, forming parallelogram regions.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="14" source="eval" puzzle_id="e3721c99" lines="69-83">
<description>
Recolor grey shapes by matching their hole count to a color legend.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color; grey (5) sprites scattered across the grid.
- Colored legend shapes (non-bg, non-grey, at least 2×2, not touching the outer border) near grid edge,
  each with a distinct number of enclosed holes.

Rule:
- Each colored legend shape defines a mapping: hole count → color.
- For every grey sprite, count its holes (enclosed bg regions not touching the sprite's bounding-box border).
- Recolor the grey sprite to the matching legend color; erase grey distractors with unlisted hole counts.

Key insight: Shape identity is encoded by topology (number of holes), not geometry or size.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="15" source="eval" puzzle_id="b10624e5" lines="24-39">
<description>
Replicate a motif across quadrants with scaling and reflection keyed to red anchor blocks.

Input structure:
- Grid divided into four quadrants by a full-row and full-column divider of a single non-bg color.
- Each quadrant contains a red (2) square anchor block of varying size.
- The top-left quadrant also has a colored motif attached to its red anchor.


